            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "This study investigates urban surface water flooding caused by extreme precipitation that exceeds drainage capacity. To evaluate flood hazards under both current and future climate conditions, this study applieded a combined modeling approach using two primary tools: HEC-HMS and HEC-RAS (Fig. 2). First, HEC-HMS transforms rainfall data into surface runoff. This runoff is then used as the input for HEC-RAS, which utilizes two-dimensional modeling to simulate how the floodwater actually moves across the landscape.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HEC-RAS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "PRODUCES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "water depth",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "By linking these processes, the framework can accurately estimate flood discharge, map the areas that will go underwater, and calculate water depth. This model was built using high-resolution elevation maps, watershed boundaries, and specific drainage points.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "HEC-HMS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Nash-Sutcliffe Efficiency",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "This study builds upon a previously developed HEC-HMS model for the Bagmati River system, which was calibrated and validated at the Khokana outlet [26]. Model calibration against observed discharge employed standard statistical performance indicators, including Nash-Sutcliffe Efficiency (NSE) and percentage bias (PBIAS), all of which indicated good agreement between simulated and observed flows.",
